# Line endings, pinned so they can never become a source of noise.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, which converts on checkout and
# back on commit. That mostly works, but it is a per-machine setting: anyone who clones
# with a different value gets different working-tree endings, and a file can then appear
# entirely rewritten in a diff when nothing about it changed. Attributes live in the repo,
# so they apply to every clone regardless of local config, and they win over autocrlf.
#
# LF everywhere, in the repo and in the working tree. Modern Windows editors (VS Code,
# Android Studio) handle LF without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception: cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The Gradle wrapper script is executed by a POSIX shell and must stay LF, even though
# the .bat beside it must not.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Binaries: never converted, never diffed as text. Git usually detects these on its own,
# but the emulator cores are the single most important thing in the tree to not mangle,
# so they are declared rather than left to heuristics.
*.so binary
*.bak binary
*.bin binary
*.img binary
*.apk binary
*.jar binary
*.aar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.chd binary
